  "response": {
    "answer": "The key paper on object detection in the workspace is *You Only Look Once: Unified, Real-Time Object Detection* by Joseph Redmon et al. This work introduces YOLO, a real-time object detection system that frames detection as a regression problem, enabling end-to-end optimization. The base model runs at 45 frames per second, while Fast YOLO achieves 155 frames per second, outperforming other real-time detectors in mAP. YOLO generalizes well to non-photographic domains like artwork, though it tends to make more localization errors compared to state-of-the-art systems.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "work_id": "da37b10b-f5e6-4b74-9659-cc66e4abec26",
        "provenance_kind": "workspace_abstract",
        "evidence_mode": "abstract",
        "excerpt": "We present YOLO, a new approach to object detection. Prior work on object detection repurposes classifiers to perform detection. Instead, we frame object detection as a regression problem to spatially separated bounding boxes and associated class probabilities. A single neural network predicts bounding boxes and class probabilities directly from full images in one evaluation. Since the whole detection pipeline is a single network, it can be optimized end-to-end directly on detection performance. Our unified architecture is extremely fast. Our base YOLO model processes images in real-time at 45 frames per second. A smaller version of the network, Fast YOLO, processes an astounding 155 frames per second while still achieving double the mAP of other real-time detectors. Compared to state-of-the-art detection systems, YOLO makes more localization errors but is less likely to predict false positives on background. Finally, YOLO learns very general representations of objects. It outperforms other detection methods, including DPM and R-CNN, when generalizing from natural images to other domains like artwork.",
        "evidence_quality": "medium",
        "is_external": false
      }
    ],
